# HexNet: High-Performance Graph Topology & Pathfinding Engine in Modern C++

Developed as a high-performance spatial network simulator in modern C++11, HexNet abstracts a non-orthogonal hexagonal coordinate system into an undirected graph to evaluate boundary-to-boundary path connectivity. Engineered using memory-efficient STL structures, the system maps out a 6-degree node adjacency network while handling edge-case boundary mutations (corners and sides). The project utilizes strict object-oriented paradigms, adheres to the Google C++ Style Guide, and generates clean system documentation via Doxygen.

While visually rendered as a strategic game simulation, the underlying graph-theory logic directly scales to complex engineering domains — including Electrical Engineering topology (power grid fault-tolerance modeling, VLSI semiconductor trace routing) and Robotics path planning (SLAM spatial discretization, kinematic map configurations, and autonomous swarm spatial tracking).

An AI opponent via Monte Carlo Simulation is additionally implemented, leveraging randomized game-tree exploration to determine statistically optimal moves against a human player. Win detection runs in near O(1) amortized time using a Union-Find (Disjoint Set) data structure with path compression and union by size — a deliberate design choice that scales efficiently under the thousands of simulations Monte Carlo requires per move.

This project serves as the final deliverable for the *Coding for Everyone: C and C++* Specialization by Ira Pohl on Coursera. Basically just a Hex Game in C++ that uses various DSA techniques — just made it sound techy. 😄

